WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.038
Picture a library with full shelves.

2
00:00:05.038 --> 00:00:09.196
A new book arrives, and one book has to go down to the basement.

3
00:00:09.196 --> 00:00:09.845
Which one?

4
00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:11.874
A cache faces the same choice.

5
00:00:11.874 --> 00:00:20.808
It keeps copies of data close by in memory, and when memory is full, it has to guess which key it will need least soon, and throw that one out.

6
00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:27.449
Every way of choosing, called an eviction policy, answers with a different kind of evidence about the past.

7
00:00:27.700 --> 00:00:34.192
FIFO, first in, first out, removes the book that arrived first, however often people read it.

8
00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:40.192
LRU, least recently used, removes the book nobody has opened for the longest.

9
00:00:40.192 --> 00:00:43.501
LFU removes the one opened the fewest times.

10
00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:45.290
LRU is the usual choice.

11
00:00:45.290 --> 00:00:47.277
Here's how it keeps its order.

12
00:00:47.277 --> 00:00:49.728
The shelf runs from oldest to newest.

13
00:00:49.728 --> 00:00:53.305
When someone reads a book, it moves to the newest end.

14
00:00:53.500 --> 00:00:56.216
And the book that goes is the one at the oldest end.

15
00:00:56.500 --> 00:01:00.496
Now the three popular books, A, B and C, get read all day.

16
00:01:00.496 --> 00:01:03.734
Then, one night, a report reads every key once.

17
00:01:03.734 --> 00:01:05.181
That's called a scan.

18
00:01:05.367 --> 00:01:07.071
Before you watch, guess.

19
00:01:07.071 --> 00:01:09.627
What does a scan do to an LRU shelf?

20
00:01:09.627 --> 00:01:10.763
Pause and guess.

21
00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:19.837
Each scan book arrives, looks like the newest thing on the shelf, and pushes the oldest one out.

22
00:01:19.837 --> 00:01:23.485
Six scan books later, every popular book is in the basement.

23
00:01:23.967 --> 00:01:26.480
Tomorrow, the first reader of A misses.

24
00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:28.865
Well, that's not the shelf we wanted.

25
00:01:29.067 --> 00:01:30.568
LRU has a second cost.

26
00:01:30.568 --> 00:01:37.595
Every hit moves a key, and the list it moves on has a lock, so only one thread at a time can change it.

27
00:01:37.595 --> 00:01:39.369
Busy threads queue for it.

28
00:01:39.667 --> 00:01:43.028
How common are books read only once?

29
00:01:43.028 --> 00:01:50.219
A 2023 study looked at 6,594 recordings of the requests real caches received.

30
00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:55.305
In the middle trace, 26 percent of keys were asked for once and never again.

31
00:01:55.305 --> 00:01:58.338
In a short stretch of a trace, 72 percent were.

32
00:01:58.338 --> 00:02:03.113
So the evidence says: drop new keys quickly, unless they prove themselves.

33
00:02:03.367 --> 00:02:09.074
SIEVE, from 2024, keeps the books in arrival order, and gives each one a sticky dot.

34
00:02:09.074 --> 00:02:12.404
A read puts a dot on the book, and moves nothing.

35
00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:16.713
To make room, a hand walks from the oldest book toward the newest.

36
00:02:16.713 --> 00:02:20.016
A book with a dot keeps its place, and loses its dot.

37
00:02:20.016 --> 00:02:22.135
The first book without a dot goes.

38
00:02:22.300 --> 00:02:25.400
In code, that dot is one visited bit per key.

39
00:02:25.700 --> 00:02:26.806
Now run the scan.

40
00:02:26.806 --> 00:02:30.577
Pause and guess first: which books end up in the basement?

41
00:02:33.833 --> 00:02:37.163
The hand spares A, clears its dot, and drops E.

42
00:02:37.163 --> 00:02:38.863
It spares B and drops F.

43
00:02:38.863 --> 00:02:42.192
It spares C, and drops S1, the first scan book.

44
00:02:42.192 --> 00:02:45.380
From then on, scan books push out scan books.

45
00:02:45.867 --> 00:02:50.908
Books A, B and C are still on the shelf, and their next reads put the dots back.

46
00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:59.906
The lesson's lab races all three on a million requests with Zipf traffic, the skewed kind where a few keys get most of the requests.

47
00:02:59.906 --> 00:03:05.315
The cache holds 10,000 keys, and we count misses, requests the cache can't answer.

48
00:03:05.500 --> 00:03:10.376
SIEVE misses least: 21.6 percent, against 26.1 for LRU.

49
00:03:10.376 --> 00:03:12.859
Then the lab mixes in scans.

50
00:03:12.859 --> 00:03:15.696
Pause and guess the last column.

51
00:03:18.433 --> 00:03:21.708
FIFO rises to 32.2, and LRU to 29.0.

52
00:03:21.708 --> 00:03:23.527
SIEVE stays at 21.6.

53
00:03:23.527 --> 00:03:31.168
Scan keys arrive without a dot, so the hand drops them before any key that was read.

54
00:03:31.367 --> 00:03:32.239
A few limits.

55
00:03:32.239 --> 00:03:37.137
With a cache of only 1,000 keys, the scans changed little for any policy.

56
00:03:37.137 --> 00:03:42.169
Every policy is a bet on the past, and your traffic decides which bet wins.

57
00:03:42.169 --> 00:03:47.671
And throwing keys away is right for a cache, and wrong for data you can't rebuild.

58
00:03:47.933 --> 00:03:51.784
Here's the heart of the lab's SIEVE, in Java and Kotlin.

59
00:03:51.784 --> 00:03:53.090
A hit sets one bit.

60
00:03:53.090 --> 00:03:56.803
The hand clears bits until it finds a key without one.

61
00:03:56.803 --> 00:04:01.891
Because a hit moves nothing, SIEVE ran at twice LRU's speed on 16 threads.

62
00:04:02.133 --> 00:04:03.894
So the moral of the story.

63
00:04:03.894 --> 00:04:07.281
LRU trusts the last read, so one scan can fool it.

64
00:04:07.281 --> 00:04:11.412
SIEVE asks a new key to prove itself once before it may stay.

65
00:04:11.700 --> 00:04:12.923
One quick check.

66
00:04:12.923 --> 00:04:15.674
The hand reaches B, and B has a dot.

67
00:04:15.674 --> 00:04:17.203
Where does B end up?

68
00:04:17.203 --> 00:04:18.425
Pause and think.

69
00:04:21.667 --> 00:04:22.939
Right where it was.

70
00:04:22.939 --> 00:04:25.416
It loses its dot and keeps its place.

71
00:04:25.416 --> 00:04:28.897
LRU would have moved it; SIEVE never moves anything.
